<DocLayout prose title="Architecture — Attestree App-Patching Platform" description="System architecture of Attestree: who connects to whom, the ingest gate with sandboxed detonation and a signed statement, the fenced feed, groups × rings with windows and freezes, the fleet CVE index and the two ways to run it." sidebarTitle={nav.title} sidebarItems={nav.items}>
  <h2>System architecture</h2>
  <p>
    Attestree is a control plane for third-party app patching on Windows fleets, built around one
    idea: <strong>attest at ingest, not after install.</strong> Most scanners inspect binaries that
    already run across a fleet; Attestree owns the catalog and the distribution layer, so every
    version is observed, signed and checked <em>before</em> an endpoint can install it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deployment is simple to firewall. Only the server accepts connections: endpoints and the
    detonation broker dial out, and installers reach the sandbox through the server, never straight
    from the internet.
  </p>

  <AttestreeSystemDiagram />

  <h3>Ingest gate</h3>
  <p>
    Every version passes the gate before it enters the catalog: winget, Chocolatey and Scoop today.
    Chocolatey and Scoop are tested with the manager’s own install, need a Hyper-V detonation host,
    and their upstream reads stay off until an operator allows that egress. Your own apps can be
    added through the API so far; cached MSIs with generated MST transforms, npm and pip are on the
    roadmap.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Fetch</strong></td><td>Retrieve the manifest and installer from the source; the installer’s SHA-256 is checked as it streams in</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Signature</strong></td><td>Check the installer’s Authenticode signature and whether the signer changed since the previous version</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Detonation</strong></td><td>Install the version in a short-lived VM cloned from the operator’s signed golden image, offline by default</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>SBOM</strong></td><td>Syft builds a CycloneDX SBOM of what the installer put on disk: an observation, not a declaration</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Statement</strong></td><td>Sign an in-toto Statement v1 in a DSSE envelope with the deployment’s own ECDSA P-256 key, stored with the SBOMs and verifiable offline</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The statement records what the deployment observed. It is not SLSA build provenance, because
    Attestree does not build the vendor’s installer.
  </p>

  <h3>Three enforcement points</h3>
  <p>For a Windows fleet the endpoint is the load-bearing enforcement point, so policy is not checked only once:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Ingest gate</strong>: detonation decides what gets attested, before anything enters the catalog.</li>
    <li><strong>Reconciliation</strong>: continuous reconciliation against declared desired state, with drift flagged in the console.</li>
    <li><strong>Endpoint agent</strong>: acts only on desired state whose HMAC checks out and, for winget on a fenced endpoint, installs only the attested SHA-256.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Fenced feed</h3>
  <p>
    Fenced endpoints run from the operator’s own attested winget source. A user can still run a
    normal <code>winget install</code>, but the agent serves only admitted packages, pinned to the
    attested SHA-256. Chocolatey and Scoop versions are served only from bytes the server holds, and
    only to endpoints whose groups name the app.
  </p>

  <h3>Groups × rings</h3>

  <AttestreeRingsDiagram />

  <p>
    <strong>PC groups</strong> carry version-free sets of packages and can mix winget, Chocolatey and
    Scoop; an endpoint converges to the union of its groups. <strong>Rings</strong> (canary → pilot →
    broad → all) resolve each package to the exact attested version at deploy time. Each ring keeps
    one armed predecessor, and rolling back makes it the ring’s version again. Maintenance windows,
    per group or per package, and dated freezes decide when endpoints install; a Critical or
    KEV-listed fix gets through within three days by default.
  </p>
  <p>
    An opt-in <strong>unattended update pipeline</strong> ingests, detonates and promotes new versions
    ring by ring, for winget and for subscribed Chocolatey and Scoop apps. It halts into a review
    queue when a publisher’s signing certificate changes or an installer arrives unsigned. Rollback
    proposals for bad CVE and slow-burn signals ship off; when on, an operator confirms each one.
  </p>

  <h3>Fleet inventory and CVE index</h3>
  <p>
    The agent reports an observed inventory across winget, Chocolatey, Scoop and the uninstall
    entries. A <strong>self-hosted CVE index</strong> matches it against the public CVE list
    (cvelistV5) and the CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue, with an offline bundle for
    air-gapped sites. A card per product leads to Deploy fix, which promotes the fixed version one
    ring per typed confirmation. The audit trail, the catalog evidence and the fleet inventory export
    as signed bundles that verify offline.
  </p>

  <h3>Control plane stack</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Choice</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Installer shim</strong></td><td>.NET Native AOT, Apache-2.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Endpoint agent</strong></td><td>.NET, self-updating through the same rings; dials out only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Server</strong></td><td>Linux container with PostgreSQL 18; row-level security and an insert-only audit log bind the platform itself</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Detonation</strong></td><td>A broker on the operator’s Hyper-V or Proxmox host, gRPC over mutual TLS; VM size and concurrency set per broker</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Formats</strong></td><td>in-toto Statement v1 in DSSE, CycloneDX, ECDSA P-256; an optional Sigstore Rekor entry, off by default</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Release signing</strong></td><td>cosign-signed images; Authenticode-signed agent and broker MSIs</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Ways to run it</h3>

  <AttestreeTopologyDiagram />

  <p>
    Attestree ships as open-core. The installer shim and its IPC contract are Apache-2.0; the
    platform is closed source, and the licence boundary is enforced in the build. The <strong>Community Edition</strong> is free up to 200 endpoints, self-hosted on Docker Compose
    with no cloud dependency, and in early access. Commercially there is a <strong>dedicated instance</strong> in the customer’s own Azure subscription, deployed and
    upgraded by Attestree. There is deliberately no shared multi-tenant SaaS.
  </p>

  <h3>Public front door</h3>
  <p>The site at attestree.com is a separate, fully public surface:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Choice</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Framework</strong></td><td>Astro with Tailwind and MDX, static output, build-time OG images</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Hosting</strong></td><td>Cloudflare Pages, with a preview deploy per pull request</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Demo</strong></td><td>A clickable, read-only copy of the running console with a guided tour, captured from the Community Edition against a seeded test fleet</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Changelog</strong></td><td>One public changelog for every release, mirrored to the Community Edition repository</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Waitlist</strong></td><td>A same-origin Cloudflare Pages Function backed by D1 in an EU jurisdiction, with Turnstile verification</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Build security</strong></td><td>SHA-pinned actions, a least-privilege token, a committed lockfile, Dependabot, an <code>npm audit</code> gate and <code>security.txt</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</DocLayout>
